  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nSurges of arctic air continue to plunge southward across the central \nUS behind area of low pressure over eastern Ohio.  The next push of \narctic air is anticipated to arrive this morning, and 925 hpa \ntemperatures are expected to fall to near 0 C.  Despite \nunidirectional winds leading to mixing to near 850 hpa, the \ncooling temperatures aloft will only lead to a five to seven \ndegree diurnal curve across northern Missouri, and closer to 10 \ndegrees of warming across central Missouri today. Lingering \nmoisture will lead to cumulostratus development east of highway \n65 which is expected to rapidly dissipate with the loss of \nsolar heating tonight. \n\nThe clearing skies tonight will lead to the coolest night of the \nseason thus far across the region with lows in the upper 20s in most \nlocations. A light north breeze through the night will push wind \nchill values into the lower 20s, perhaps the upper teens in isolated \nlocations. \n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to build into the region on \nFriday keeping temperatures below seasonal norms for this time of \nyear. Clear skies and light winds Friday night into Saturday morning \nwill once again lead to the coolest temperatures of the season, and \na hard freeze region-wide (50-90% chance of temperatures less \nthan 25 degrees F, greatest east of I-35). \n\nShortwave building east from the west coast will bring a chance of \nrain to the southern plains on Sunday, but dry airmass in place will \nlikely keep the region dry through the weekend and keep any \nprecipitation chances south. \n\nTemperatures return closer to seasonal norms early next week with \nomega block developing across the central US keeping the region dry \nthrough the first half of the week.  West coast trough eventually \nweakens and lifts northeast, potentially creating a chance of light \nrain late next week.",
